DEEP[1]=("Chapter 13 is named Kṣetra-Kṣetrajña-Vibhāga Yoga, the yoga of distinguishing the field and its knower. It opens the last six chapters, often called the jñāna ṣaṭka.",
 "<i>Idaṃ śarīraṃ kaunteya</i>, “this body, son of Kuntī”. <i>Kṣetram ity abhidhīyate</i>, “is called the field”. <i>Etad yo vetti taṃ prāhuḥ</i>, “one who knows it, they call”. <i>Kṣetrajña iti tad-vidaḥ</i>, “the knower of the field, those who know”. A field is where seeds are sown and crops reaped; the body is where actions are sown and their fruits reaped. Śaṅkara says the knower is pure consciousness, the witness. Rāmānuja says the knower is the individual self, distinct from the body.",
 "Sit for a moment and notice: there is the body, and there is the one aware of it.")
DEEP[2]=("<i>Kṣetrajñaṃ cāpi māṃ viddhi</i>, “know me also as the knower”. <i>Sarva-kṣetreṣu bhārata</i>, “in all fields, Bhārata”. <i>Kṣetra-kṣetrajñayor jñānaṃ</i>, “knowledge of the field and its knower”. <i>Yat taj jñānaṃ mataṃ mama</i>, “that, I hold, is knowledge”.",
 "This verse is central to the debate between the schools. Śaṅkara: the individual knower and the Lord are one; difference is due to ignorance. Rāmānuja: the Lord is the inner self of every individual self, as the self is the inner self of the body. Madhva: the Lord dwells in every field as controller, eternally distinct from the individual knower. All agree knowledge of this is true knowledge.",
 "The awareness that looks through your eyes looks through every eye. Let that change how you see others.")
DEEP[3]=("<i>Tat kṣetraṃ yac ca yādṛk ca</i>, “what that field is and what it is like”. <i>Yad-vikāri yataś ca yat</i>, “its changes and its source”. <i>Sa ca yo yat-prabhāvaś ca</i>, “and who he is and what his powers”. <i>Tat samāsena me śṛṇu</i>, “hear that from me in brief”.",
 "Commentators note that chapter 13 is the Gītā’s most philosophical chapter, close to the language of Sāṅkhya and the Upaniṣads. The outline here is followed in order: the field (13.5–6), its marks of knowledge (13.7–11), the knowable (13.12–17), and the relation of nature and spirit (13.19–34).",
 "Before solving a problem, list its parts. Clarity of structure brings clarity of thought.")
DEEP[4]=("<i>Ṛṣibhir bahudhā gītaṃ</i>, “sung by sages in many ways”. <i>Chandobhir vividhaiḥ pṛthak</i>, “in various distinct hymns”. <i>Brahma-sūtra-padaiś caiva</i>, “and in the words of the Brahma-sūtras”. <i>Hetumadbhir viniścitaiḥ</i>, “well-reasoned and conclusive”.",
 "The Brahma Sūtras of Bādarāyaṇa, the Upaniṣads and the Gītā form the <i>prasthāna-traya</i>, the three foundations of Vedānta; every major teacher wrote commentaries on all three. Rāmānuja takes <i>brahma-sūtra</i> as Bādarāyaṇa’s text. Some scholars think the Gītā predates the Sūtras in their present form; the Sūtras themselves seem to refer to the Gītā as <i>smṛti</i>.",
 "Good teaching satisfies both reverence and reason. Look for both in what you learn.")
DEEP[5]=("<i>Mahā-bhūtāny ahaṅkāro</i>, “the great elements, ego”. <i>Buddhir avyaktam eva ca</i>, “intellect and the unmanifest”. <i>Indriyāṇi daśaikaṃ ca</i>, “the ten senses and the one”. <i>Pañca cendriya-gocarāḥ</i>, “and the five objects of the senses”.",
 "Classical Sāṅkhya, as in Īśvarakṛṣṇa’s Sāṅkhya Kārikā, counts twenty-five principles: twenty-four of matter and <i>puruṣa</i>, spirit. The Gītā lists the twenty-four as the field. The five senses of knowing are hearing, touch, sight, taste and smell; the five of action are speech, hands, feet, excretion and generation. Commentators stress that mind, intellect and ego are on the field’s side.",
 "Your thoughts and your sense of “I” belong to the field. The knower watches them.")
DEEP[6]=("<i>Icchā dveṣaḥ sukhaṃ duḥkhaṃ</i>, “desire, aversion, pleasure, pain”. <i>Saṅghātaś cetanā dhṛtiḥ</i>, “the body, awareness, steadfastness”. <i>Etat kṣetraṃ samāsena</i>, “this in brief is the field”. <i>Sa-vikāram udāhṛtam</i>, “with its modifications”.",
 "<i>Saṅghāta</i>, the aggregate, is the body as a combination of parts. The Nyāya school listed desire, aversion, pleasure and pain as marks of the self; the Gītā firmly places them in the field. Śaṅkara says they are known, so they cannot be the knower. Rāmānuja says they are the products of the field acting on the self.",
 "Moods come and go; you notice them. That noticing is the first step to freedom from them.")
DEEP[7]=("<i>Amānitvam adambhitvam</i>, “humility, unpretentiousness”. <i>Ahiṃsā kṣāntir ārjavam</i>, “non-violence, patience, uprightness”. <i>Ācāryopāsanaṃ śaucaṃ</i>, “service of the teacher, purity”. <i>Sthairyam ātma-vinigrahaḥ</i>, “steadfastness, self-control”.",
 "Verses 7–11 list twenty marks of knowledge, and the list begins with humility. <i>Amānitva</i> is not thinking highly of oneself; <i>adambhitva</i> is not pretending to be what one is not. <i>Ārjava</i>, straightness, is honesty in thought, word and deed. <i>Ācāryopāsana</i>, serving the teacher, recalls 4.34. Gandhi and Vinoba made these qualities ashram vows.",
 "Real knowledge shows first in humility. Notice whether your learning makes you gentler.")
DEEP[8]=("<i>Indriyārtheṣu vairāgyam</i>, “dispassion toward sense objects”. <i>Anahaṅkāra eva ca</i>, “and absence of ego”. <i>Janma-mṛtyu-jarā-vyādhi</i>, “birth, death, old age, sickness”. <i>Duḥkha-doṣānudarśanam</i>, “seeing the evil of pain in them”.",
 "The young prince Siddhārtha, the future Buddha, saw an old man, a sick man, a corpse and a renunciant, and left his palace. Commentators often draw the parallel. Śaṅkara explains <i>anudarśana</i> as reflecting again and again on these facts until they loosen attachment. Doctors and nurses who see suffering daily sometimes say this verse describes their education too.",
 "Facing ageing and illness honestly makes us wiser and kinder. Don’t look away.")
DEEP[9]=("<i>Asaktir anabhiṣvaṅgaḥ</i>, “non-attachment, not clinging”. <i>Putra-dāra-gṛhādiṣu</i>, “to son, wife, home and the like”. <i>Nityaṃ ca sama-cittatvam</i>, “and constant evenness of mind”. <i>Iṣṭāniṣṭopapattiṣu</i>, “in desired and undesired events”.",
 "<i>Asakti</i> is not being attached; <i>anabhiṣvaṅga</i> is not over-identifying. Śaṅkara’s example: one who weeps when another’s child is happy or sad as if it were his own has <i>abhiṣvaṅga</i>. Commentators stress that the Gītā does not ask us to stop loving family, but to love without the anxious grip that makes us suffer and others feel smothered.",
 "Love fully, hold lightly. It is better for you and for those you love.")
DEEP[10]=("<i>Mayi cānanya-yogena</i>, “and to me, with undivided yoga”. <i>Bhaktir avyabhicāriṇī</i>, “unwavering devotion”. <i>Vivikta-deśa-sevitvam</i>, “resorting to solitary places”. <i>Aratir jana-saṃsadi</i>, “distaste for crowds”.",
 "<i>Avyabhicāriṇī bhakti</i>, devotion that does not stray, is listed among the marks of knowledge. Commentators note this as one sign that the Gītā does not separate devotion from wisdom. Śaṅkara takes devotion here as fixed meditation on the Lord as one’s Self. <i>Jana-saṃsad</i>, crowds or worldly gatherings, means idle company, not service of people.",
 "Make some time each day to be alone with yourself and what you hold sacred.")
DEEP[11]=("<i>Adhyātma-jñāna-nityatvaṃ</i>, “constancy in knowledge of the Self”. <i>Tattva-jñānārtha-darśanam</i>, “seeing the goal of true knowledge”. <i>Etaj jñānam iti proktam</i>, “this is called knowledge”. <i>Ajñānaṃ yad ato’nyathā</i>, “what is contrary is ignorance”.",
 "The list ends with a bold definition. Commentators note the Gītā’s approach: knowledge is a way of being. Śaṅkara says these qualities are called knowledge because they lead to it and are its fruit. The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad calls the knowledge of the Imperishable “higher knowledge”, and all other learning “lower”. A learned person who is arrogant, the Gītā implies, is still in ignorance.",
 "Measure your knowledge by your character. Learning without kindness is not yet wisdom.")
DEEP[12]=("<i>Jñeyaṃ yat tat pravakṣyāmi</i>, “I will tell what is to be known”. <i>Yaj jñātvāmṛtam aśnute</i>, “knowing which, one tastes immortality”. <i>Anādimat paraṃ brahma</i>, “the beginningless supreme Brahman”. <i>Na sat tan nāsad ucyate</i>, “it is called neither being nor non-being”.",
 "<i>Na sat na asat</i> echoes the Nāsadīya Sūkta (Ṛg Veda 10.129.1): “there was neither non-being nor being then”. Śaṅkara says Brahman cannot be called “being” in the sense of an object, because it is the subject. Rāmānuja reads <i>anādimat param</i> as “the beginningless one who has me as supreme”, keeping the Lord above.",
 "Some truths are beyond yes and no. Paradox is a way of pointing past words.")
DEEP[13]=("<i>Sarvataḥ pāṇi-pādaṃ tat</i>, “with hands and feet everywhere”. <i>Sarvato’kṣi-śiro-mukham</i>, “eyes, heads and mouths everywhere”. <i>Sarvataḥ śrutimal loke</i>, “ears everywhere in the world”. <i>Sarvam āvṛtya tiṣṭhati</i>, “it dwells, enveloping all”.",
 "This verse is nearly identical to Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad 3.16, and close to the Puruṣa Sūkta’s thousand heads and eyes. Commentators explain that Brahman has no hands of its own, yet all hands act by its power. Śaṅkara calls this a description through limiting adjuncts. Rāmānuja says the Lord pervades all as their inner self.",
 "Every helping hand you see is, in a sense, the Divine at work. Be one.")
DEEP[14]=("<i>Sarvendriya-guṇābhāsaṃ</i>, “shining through the functions of all senses”. <i>Sarvendriya-vivarjitam</i>, “yet free of all senses”. <i>Asaktaṃ sarva-bhṛc caiva</i>, “unattached, yet supporting all”. <i>Nirguṇaṃ guṇa-bhoktṛ ca</i>, “beyond the guṇas, yet their experiencer”.",
 "This verse echoes Śvetāśvatara 3.17. <i>Nirguṇa</i>, beyond the guṇas, is a key word in Advaita; many Hindus speak of God as <i>nirguṇa</i> (without qualities) and <i>saguṇa</i> (with qualities). Rāmānuja reads <i>nirguṇa</i> as free from the material guṇas, while full of auspicious divine qualities. Paradox is the Gītā’s way of pointing to what is beyond ordinary categories.",
 "Awareness is in every experience, yet is none of them. Try noticing that during a meal.")
DEEP[15]=("<i>Bahir antaś ca bhūtānām</i>, “outside and inside beings”. <i>Acaraṃ caram eva ca</i>, “unmoving and moving”. <i>Sūkṣmatvāt tad avijñeyaṃ</i>, “too subtle to be known”. <i>Dūra-sthaṃ cāntike ca tat</i>, “far away, and near”.",
 "The Īśā Upaniṣad (5) says: <i>tad ejati tan naijati tad dūre tad v antike</i>, “it moves, it moves not; it is far, it is near”. Commentators say it is far for the ignorant, who look outside; near for the wise, who find it as their own Self. The Muṇḍaka (3.1.7) adds: farther than the far, yet here, hidden in the cave of the heart.",
 "What seems farthest may be closer than your breath. Look inward.")
DEEP[16]=("<i>Avibhaktaṃ ca bhūteṣu</i>, “undivided among beings”. <i>Vibhaktam iva ca sthitam</i>, “yet standing as if divided”. <i>Bhūta-bhartṛ ca taj jñeyaṃ</i>, “and it is to be known as sustainer of beings”. <i>Grasiṣṇu prabhaviṣṇu ca</i>, “devourer and creator”.",
 "The image of one sun reflected in many pots is used by Śaṅkara and later Advaitins. When the pots break, the reflections return to the one sun. Rāmānuja prefers the image of one soul animating many limbs. The triad of creator, sustainer and devourer is later personified as Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Śiva.",
 "Beneath our differences lies one reality. Let that deepen your respect for others.")
DEEP[17]=("<i>Jyotiṣām api taj jyotis</i>, “it is the light even of lights”. <i>Tamasaḥ param ucyate</i>, “said to be beyond darkness”. <i>Jñānaṃ jñeyaṃ jñāna-gamyaṃ</i>, “knowledge, the knowable, the goal of knowledge”. <i>Hṛdi sarvasya viṣṭhitam</i>, “seated in the hearts of all”.",
 "The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (4.4.16) calls Brahman <i>jyotiṣāṃ jyotiḥ</i>, “light of lights”. The Kaṭha (2.2.15) and Muṇḍaka (2.2.10) say the sun does not shine there; by its light all this shines. Commentators note the threefold phrase: Brahman is knowledge itself, the object of knowledge and its goal. <i>Hṛdi sarvasya</i>, in everyone’s heart, recalls 10.20 and 15.15.",
 "The light you seek is the light you see with. Turn toward it.")
DEEP[18]=("<i>Iti kṣetraṃ tathā jñānaṃ</i>, “thus the field and knowledge”. <i>Jñeyaṃ coktaṃ samāsataḥ</i>, “and the knowable, told in brief”. <i>Mad-bhakta etad vijñāya</i>, “my devotee, understanding this”. <i>Mad-bhāvāyopapadyate</i>, “becomes fit for my state”.",
 "This verse closes the first half of the chapter. Commentators note that even after the most abstract teaching, Kṛṣṇa says “my devotee”. Śaṅkara says the devotee who knows this reaches oneness with the Lord. Rāmānuja says such a devotee reaches a state like the Lord’s, free from matter.",
 "Knowledge and love are partners. Let what you learn deepen what you love.")
DEEP[19]=("<i>Prakṛtiṃ puruṣaṃ caiva</i>, “nature and spirit”. <i>Viddhy anādī ubhāv api</i>, “know both to be beginningless”. <i>Vikārāṃś ca guṇāṃś caiva</i>, “and modifications and guṇas”. <i>Viddhi prakṛti-sambhavān</i>, “know them as born of nature”.",
 "In classical Sāṅkhya, <i>prakṛti</i> and many <i>puruṣas</i> are two independent eternal realities, with no God above them. The Gītā uses the same terms but places both under the Lord: they are his lower and higher natures (7.4–5). Śaṅkara says both are the Lord’s powers. Rāmānuja says both are the Lord’s body.",
 "You are a spirit living in nature. Both are older than any story you know.")
DEEP[20]=("<i>Kārya-kāraṇa-kartṛtve</i>, “in the making of effects and instruments”. <i>Hetuḥ prakṛtir ucyate</i>, “nature is said to be the cause”. <i>Puruṣaḥ sukha-duḥkhānāṃ</i>, “spirit, of pleasures and pains”. <i>Bhoktṛtve hetur ucyate</i>, “is said to be the cause of experiencing”.",
 "Śaṅkara reads <i>kārya</i> as the body and <i>karaṇa</i> as the senses. Nature produces them, and spirit experiences what they bring. Sāṅkhya uses the image of a lame man riding on a blind man’s shoulders: the blind one walks (nature), the lame one sees (spirit), and together they move. Confusing the walker and the seer is bondage.",
 "Your body acts; you experience. Watching both creates a little space for wisdom.")
DEEP[21]=("<i>Puruṣaḥ prakṛti-stho hi</i>, “spirit, seated in nature”. <i>Bhuṅkte prakṛti-jān guṇān</i>, “experiences the guṇas born of nature”. <i>Kāraṇaṃ guṇa-saṅgo’sya</i>, “attachment to the guṇas is the cause”. <i>Sad-asad-yoni-janmasu</i>, “of its births in good and evil wombs”.",
 "<i>Guṇa-saṅga</i>, attachment to the guṇas, is named as the root of rebirth. Chapter 14 will explain how each guṇa binds: sattva by attachment to happiness and knowledge, rajas by attachment to action, tamas by negligence and sleep (14.6–8). Commentators note that the spirit is not bound by nature but by its own clinging.",
 "It is not the world that binds us but our clinging to it. Loosen one small grip today.")
DEEP[22]=("<i>Upadraṣṭānumantā ca</i>, “witness and permitter”. <i>Bhartā bhoktā maheśvaraḥ</i>, “supporter, experiencer, great Lord”. <i>Paramātmeti cāpy ukto</i>, “and also called the supreme Self”. <i>Dehe’smin puruṣaḥ paraḥ</i>, “the supreme Person in this body”.",
 "The Muṇḍaka (3.1.1) and Śvetāśvatara (4.6) Upaniṣads give the image of two birds on one tree: one eats the fruit, the other watches. Commentators link the two: the experiencing self and the witnessing Supreme dwell together in the body. Śaṅkara says the witness and the experiencer are ultimately one. Rāmānuja and Madhva say they are two, friends on the same tree.",
 "Within you is a quiet witness who sees everything and judges gently. Rest in that presence.")
DEEP[23]=("<i>Ya evaṃ vetti puruṣaṃ</i>, “one who knows spirit thus”. <i>Prakṛtiṃ ca guṇaiḥ saha</i>, “and nature with the guṇas”. <i>Sarvathā vartamāno’pi</i>, “however he may live”. <i>Na sa bhūyo’bhijāyate</i>, “is not born again”.",
 "<i>Sarvathā vartamāno’pi</i> echoes 6.31. Commentators warn against misreading it as permission for any conduct. Śaṅkara says one who truly knows the Self cannot act from selfish desire. Rāmānuja says such a person, whatever their station in life, is freed. The point is that freedom does not depend on outer circumstances: a king, a farmer or a monk can know the truth.",
 "Freedom is possible in any life. Don’t wait for perfect circumstances.")
DEEP[24]=("<i>Dhyānenātmani paśyanti</i>, “by meditation they see in the self”. <i>Kecid ātmānam ātmanā</i>, “some, the Self by the self”. <i>Anye sāṅkhyena yogena</i>, “others by the yoga of knowledge”. <i>Karma-yogena cāpare</i>, “and others by the yoga of action”.",
 "Three paths, each with a chapter of the Gītā: meditation (chapter 6), knowledge (chapter 2) and action (chapter 3). Commentators note the Gītā’s openness to different temperaments. Śaṅkara says the paths are for seekers at different stages. Rāmānuja says karma yoga leads to knowledge, and knowledge to meditation. The next verse adds a fourth path for those who simply trust.",
 "Find the path that suits your nature, and walk it steadily.")
DEEP[25]=("<i>Anye tv evam ajānantaḥ</i>, “others, not knowing this”. <i>Śrutvānyebhya upāsate</i>, “hearing from others, worship”. <i>Te’pi cātitaranty eva</i>, “they too cross over”. <i>Mṛtyuṃ śruti-parāyaṇāḥ</i>, “death, devoted to what they heard”.",
 "Commentators call this a verse for the ordinary believer. <i>Śruti-parāyaṇa</i>, “devoted to what is heard”: one who trusts and follows the wise. Śaṅkara says such people cross death gradually, through faith and practice. Rāmānuja says hearing with faith leads to devotion. Many people today meet the Gītā through talks, songs and stories; this verse honours that path.",
 "You don’t need to know everything. Trust the wise, follow sincerely, and keep going.")
DEEP[26]=("<i>Yāvat sañjāyate kiñcit</i>, “whatever being is born”. <i>Sattvaṃ sthāvara-jaṅgamam</i>, “moving or unmoving”. <i>Kṣetra-kṣetrajña-saṃyogāt</i>, “from the union of field and knower”. <i>Tad viddhi bharatarṣabha</i>, “know that, bull of the Bharatas”.",
 "Commentators explain that the union is not physical joining. Śaṅkara calls it mutual superimposition: the qualities of the body are wrongly attributed to the Self, and the consciousness of the Self to the body, like a crystal appearing red near a red flower. Rāmānuja calls it a real but temporary association of self and matter, caused by karma.",
 "Every living being is awareness in a body. Respect both sides of that union.")
DEEP[27]=("<i>Samaṃ sarveṣu bhūteṣu</i>, “equally in all beings”. <i>Tiṣṭhantaṃ parameśvaram</i>, “the supreme Lord dwelling”. <i>Vinaśyatsv avinaśyantaṃ</i>, “the imperishable in the perishing”. <i>Yaḥ paśyati sa paśyati</i>, “who sees thus, truly sees”.",
 "<i>Yaḥ paśyati sa paśyati</i> repeats 5.5. Commentators of all schools treasure this verse. Śaṅkara says the one Self is seen in all bodies. Rāmānuja says the Lord is seen as the inner controller of all. Madhva says the Lord is seen as present everywhere. The phrase “the imperishable in the perishing” recalls 2.18 and 8.20.",
 "Look for the same light in every face, including the difficult ones.")
DEEP[28]=("<i>Samaṃ paśyan hi sarvatra</i>, “seeing equally everywhere”. <i>Samavasthitam īśvaram</i>, “the Lord equally present”. <i>Na hinasty ātmanātmānaṃ</i>, “he does not harm the Self by the self”. <i>Tato yāti parāṃ gatim</i>, “so he reaches the supreme goal”.",
 "Commentators read <i>na hinasti ātmanā ātmānam</i> in two ways. One: seeing the Lord in all, one does not harm others, who are oneself. Two: seeing truly, one no longer harms one’s own Self by ignorance, which is a kind of spiritual suicide. The Īśā Upaniṣad (3) speaks of those who “slay the Self” by living in darkness. Gandhi read this verse as a basis for non-violence.",
 "Harming others harms us too. Seeing the unity makes kindness natural.")
DEEP[29]=("<i>Prakṛtyaiva ca karmāṇi</i>, “by nature alone, actions”. <i>Kriyamāṇāni sarvaśaḥ</i>, “are done in every way”. <i>Yaḥ paśyati tathātmānam</i>, “who sees, and the Self”. <i>Akartāraṃ sa paśyati</i>, “as not the doer, truly sees”.",
 "Commentators link this verse with 3.27, 5.8–9 and 18.16. Śaṅkara says the Self is never the doer; this knowledge frees from merit and sin. Rāmānuja says the self acts only through the body, which belongs to nature. The practical fruit is freedom from pride when things go well and from crushing guilt when they go badly, while still taking responsibility.",
 "Watch your actions happen with a little distance. It frees you from both pride and needless guilt.")
DEEP[30]=("<i>Yadā bhūta-pṛthag-bhāvam</i>, “when the separate states of beings”. <i>Eka-stham anupaśyati</i>, “one sees as resting in the One”. <i>Tata eva ca vistāraṃ</i>, “and their spreading out from it”. <i>Brahma sampadyate tadā</i>, “then one attains Brahman”.",
 "The image of a tree: many branches, one trunk. Commentators note two movements: seeing the many resting in the One, and seeing them spread from the One. Śaṅkara says when all is seen as the Self, one becomes Brahman. Rāmānuja says when all is seen as the Lord’s body, one reaches Brahman. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad’s teaching that all clay pots are clay is often cited.",
 "Look at the variety around you, then look for the root that holds it all.")
DEEP[31]=("<i>Anāditvān nirguṇatvāt</i>, “being beginningless and without guṇas”. <i>Paramātmāyam avyayaḥ</i>, “this imperishable supreme Self”. <i>Śarīra-stho’pi kaunteya</i>, “though dwelling in the body, son of Kuntī”. <i>Na karoti na lipyate</i>, “neither acts nor is stained”.",
 "<i>Na lipyate</i>, “is not stained”, recalls the lotus leaf of 5.10. Commentators say that because the Self has no beginning, it has no cause that could change it; because it has no qualities, nothing can cling to it. Śaṅkara takes this as a description of the Self in every being. Rāmānuja applies it to the individual self in its pure nature.",
 "At your core, nothing you have done can stain what you are. Let that free you to do better.")
DEEP[32]=("<i>Yathā sarva-gataṃ saukṣmyād</i>, “as the all-pervading, being subtle”. <i>Ākāśaṃ nopalipyate</i>, “space is not stained”. <i>Sarvatrāvasthito dehe</i>, “so, dwelling everywhere in the body”. <i>Tathātmā nopalipyate</i>, “the Self is not stained”.",
 "<i>Ākāśa</i>, space or ether, is the subtlest of the five elements. Smoke, dust and fragrance pass through it; when they clear, space is unchanged. Commentators love the image. Gauḍapāda’s Māṇḍūkya Kārikā (3.3–7) uses the pot-space image: space inside a pot seems separate, but when the pot breaks, it is one with all space.",
 "Whatever passes through you, your deepest self stays clear. Let difficulties move through like weather.")
DEEP[33]=("<i>Yathā prakāśayaty ekaḥ</i>, “as the one sun lights up”. <i>Kṛtsnaṃ lokam imaṃ raviḥ</i>, “this whole world”. <i>Kṣetraṃ kṣetrī tathā kṛtsnaṃ</i>, “so the knower of the field, the whole field”. <i>Prakāśayati bhārata</i>, “lights up, Bhārata”.",
 "The sun lights up clean and dirty places alike, and is affected by neither. Commentators apply this to awareness: it lights up all experiences, pleasant and painful, without being changed. Śaṅkara stresses that there is one sun but many objects lit. Rāmānuja says each self lights up its own body by consciousness. The image closes the chapter’s series of similes: space, sun, then the eye of knowledge.",
 "Your awareness is the sun of your life. Let it shine clearly on whatever comes.")
DEEP[34]=("<i>Kṣetra-kṣetrajñayor evam</i>, “thus of the field and its knower”. <i>Antaraṃ jñāna-cakṣuṣā</i>, “the difference, by the eye of knowledge”. <i>Bhūta-prakṛti-mokṣaṃ ca</i>, “and freedom from the nature of beings”. <i>Ye vidur yānti te param</i>, “those who know, reach the Supreme”.",
 "<i>Viveka</i>, discrimination between the seer and the seen, is the core practice of Advaita, as in Śaṅkara’s Vivekacūḍāmaṇi, “crest-jewel of discrimination”. Commentators note that the “eye of knowledge” links with the divine eye of chapter 11: there Arjuna saw the cosmos in God; here he learns to see God in himself. The colophon names chapter 13 Kṣetra-Kṣetrajña-Vibhāga Yoga.",
 "Practise telling apart what you have from what you are. It is the beginning of freedom.")
